  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will bring lots of dry weather, \nbreezy conditions at times, and seasonable temperatures through \nmid next week. \n\nA highly amplified mid/upper ridge extending across the central\nU.S. northward into Canada will persist while an upper low \nembedded in very dry air moves southward along the SC coast \ntoday. Other than some moisture in the low-levels due to a NEly \nfetch the remainder of the column will be very dry through the \nweekend and upcoming week. The area of surface high pressure \ncoupled with a broad area of low pressure far off the southeast \nU.S. coast will result in a tightened pressure gradient allowing\nfor breezy conditions at times Saturday and Sunday, especially \nfor coastal areas. The NE trajectory could allow some showers to\napproach the coast, but expect most of the shallow convection \nto remain off the coast this weekend. Highs will likely run near\nor slightly above normal through the forecast period with lows \nslightly above normal at the coast to at or below normal for the\ninland locations. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor coastal flooding and hazardous \nmarine/surf conditions are expected through at least early next \nweek. \n\nAn extended period of enhanced northeast winds due to strong \nhigh pressure to the north and low pressure developing near The \nBahamas will lead to hazardous marine/surf conditions. Also, \nincreasing astronomical tides and NE winds will lead to minor \ncoastal flooding.\n\nCoastal Flooding: Minor coastal flooding is expected to \ncontinue along the lower Cape Fear River during each high tide \ncycle, initially along the SE NC coast and lower Cape Fear River\nand then spreading southward along the NE SC coast with \ntomorrow morning's high tide. The coastal flooding will likely \npersist with each high tide into early next week. \n\nHigh Surf: Could see surf heights approach advisory thresholds \nthis weekend. The waves coupled with higher water levels could \nresult in some erosion as well, especially for E facing beaches.\n\nMarine: See the Marine section below for details regarding \nmarine hazards.",
